This assumes the standard benchmark-game reading of the problem: N is the size of the finite matrix, and the power method runs 10 iterations. The wording "N iterations" would mean 4000 or more iterations on an unspecified matrix size, which is not feasible. I have not run the code or checked its output. For N=100 the standard result is 1.274219991.

Each pass computes A·x or Aᵀ·x directly, so the full matrix is never stored. The denominator is updated incrementally as j advances, which avoids a multiplication per entry.

```php
<?php
function mulAv($n, $v) {
    $r = array_fill(0, $n, 0.0);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
        $d = ($i * ($i + 1) >> 1) + $i + 1;
        $s = 0.0;
        $step = $i + 1;
        for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j++) {
            $s += $v[$j] / $d;
            $d += $step++;
        }
        $r[$i] = $s;
    }
    return $r;
}
function mulAtv($n, $v) {
    $r = array_fill(0, $n, 0.0);
    for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
        $d = ($i * ($i + 1) >> 1) + 1 + 0; // j=0: (i)(i+1)/2 + 0 + 1
        $s = 0.0;
        $step = $i + 2;
        for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j++) {
            $s += $v[$j] / $d;
            $d += $step++;
        }
        $r[$i] = $s;
    }
    return $r;
}
$n = (int)$argv[1];
$u = array_fill(0, $n, 1.0);
$v = $u;
for ($k = 0; $k < 10; $k++) {
    $v = mulAtv($n, mulAv($n, $u));
    $u = mulAtv($n, mulAv($n, $v));
}
$vBv = 0.0; $vv = 0.0;
for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
    $vBv += $u[$i] * $v[$i];
    $vv += $v[$i] * $v[$i];
}
printf("%0.9f\n", sqrt($vBv / $vv));
```

